experience in disaster risk reduction, climate change adaptation, environmental management, development planning or related fields; technical experience working with all spheres of government in the development, planning and implementation of policy relating to biodiversity, climate change and ecosystem-based disaster risk reduction; experience in mainstreaming environmental and climate resilience considerations into municipal and sector planning, policy and budgeting processes; understanding of global and national biodiversity conservation, climate change and disaster management policies and legislation; sound understanding of disaster management Early Warning Systems (EWS) and their role in disaster preparedness and response; project, financial and risk management skills; experience in managing formal inter-institutional agreements, including MOAs or equivalent arrangements, with national or provincial partners; familiarity with Green Climate Fund or equivalent international climate fund reporting requirements will be an advantage; excellent written and verbal English communication skills, including report writing, presentation, interpersonal and workshop facilitation skills; ability to influence, motivate and collaborate with others; advanced knowledge of compliance and risk management techniques, including budget and financial management skills; proven ability to work under pressure with diverse teams while remaining cost, quality and safety conscious; ability to develop partnerships and manage multi-faceted stakeholders from various backgrounds and cultures; and advanced planning, organising and problem-solving skills.
Key Performance Responsibilities include:
Provide strategic leadership, partner management and overall Component 2 delivery;
Manage and provide oversight of the disaster preparedness and early warning systems workstream under Component 2;
Lead the mainstreaming of Eco-DRR interventions into municipal disaster risk management, asset risk management, environmental policy and spatial planning, while promoting coordination across project components;
Coordinate and provide strategic oversight of the municipal capacity-building and institutional strengthening programme under Component 2 and beyond; and
Contribute to cross-cutting project governance, monitoring and evaluation, institutional learning post project sustainability

KEY DELIVERABLES
The Council of the NHBRC is seeking to recruit an experienced Chief Executive Officer with an outstanding record of success. The person appointed to this position would report to the Council of the NHBRC.
The following, amongst others, will be key deliverables:
Overall strategic and operational responsibility for the direction and performance of the NHBRC;
Lead and guide development and implementation of the organisational strategy and plans in line with the NHBRC strategic objectives;
Working closely with the NHBRC Council and EXCO, to translate broad strategic direction into measurable outcomes for the NHBRC;
Provide effective, efficient and dynamic leadership to manage the NHBRC, in line with its legislative mandate while ensuring sound financial management and excellent corporate governance;
Sustaining and improving the goodwill already existing with the Department of Human Settlements and other stakeholders;
Identifying and implementing strategic projects/programmes that will enhance contribution to the social and economic development of marginalized groups;
Overseeing the development and implementation of effective and efficient enterprise-wide risk-mitigation, governance and management processes;
Accountable to implement risk intelligence processes with the Chief Risk Officer, Executives and the Risk Management Committee and other Governance structures;
Facilitating organisational transformation and process optimization;
Promoting conditions for continuous improvement and a healthy learning organisational culture; and
Promoting good governance and high standards of ethics to ensure a good reputation.
MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS:
Honours degree or equivalent NQF level 8 qualifications in either Engineering (or related technical disciplines) preferred, or in Law, Business Management /Administration or Finance;
Master’s Degree or equivalent NQF level 9 qualification relating to the above fields as an added advantage;
A minimum of 8 years’ operational experience at an executive level of which at least 3 years should be in government or a state owned entity.
SKILLS AND COMPETENCIES:
A strategic thinker with high ethical standards;
Proven strong leadership and management capabilities;
An understanding of the management of an extensive financial portfolio;
The capability to grasp highly technical information within the built environment;
An understanding of the prescripts detailed in the PFMA and best-in-practice corporate governance literature;
Demonstrated proficiency to deal effectively with a broad spectrum of stakeholders.
CLOSING DATE: 03 August 2026

Job Description
Our Trade Mark Enforcement Department has an exciting opportunity for an Associate to join our established and growing practice. We are looking for a confident attorney with excellent communication skills, strong attention to detail, and a passion for Trade Mark law. If you enjoy complex enforcement matters, thrive in a collaborative environment, and are ready to take the next step in your Intellectual Property career, we would love to hear from you.
Key responsibilities of the TM Enforcement Associate will include, but are not limited to:
Drafting legal documents i.e., opinions, contracts, agreements, letters of demand and pleadings;
Drafting of articles and creation of presentations, all relating to contribution towards firm marketing efforts;
Monthly invoicing of fees and disbursements;
Compilation of quotes;
File and document management (uploading documents, correspondence, managing/ monitoring deadlines, diarising files and ensuring timeous attendance, court online processes);
Liaising with clients, counter parties and agents;
Attending consultations with counsel and clients;
Assistance in the day to day running of the practice;
Attendance at client and marketing events, including presenting on topical issues from time to time
Minimum requirements:
Completed LLB with 2 years of Clerkship served in an IP firm;
Admitted Attorney;
At least 2 to 3 years PQE in an IP firm.
Completion of at least Group 1 SAIIPL Trade mark exams;
Knowledge and understanding of Trade Marks is essential;
Experience in and ability to do legal research and draft opinions;
Fully computer literate on MS Office (Knowledge Patricia and Elite 3E will be advantageous);
Excellent written and oral communication skills and good command of English;
Must be able to multi-task;
Able to work independently;
Deadline driven;
Good team player;
Good interpersonal skills;
Personal development abilities;
Client focused;
Good negotiation skills;
Able to maintain an orderly working environment;
Punctual and reliable;
Good work ethics.

Detailed description
The successful candidate will be responsible for the following key performance areas:
Draft prudential policy papers, policy positions and regulatory instruments.
Implement the Prudential Authority’s (PA) strategic objectives.
Analyse the relevance/appropriateness of international standards on highly specialised fields in banking, insurance and financial market infrastructures, with a specific focus on financial market infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk.
Develop and refine policy tools/frameworks on banking, insurance, financial market infrastructures and all related work in terms of the Financial Sector Regulation Act 9 of 2017 (FSR Act), with a specific focus on financial market infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk.
Administer and implement prudential and joint policy and regulatory instruments.
Design and implement cooperation and collaboration mechanisms with the SARB for the purposes of financial stability.
Analyse domestic and global regulations and the impact of these on financial market infrastructures.
Monitor global prudential policy implementation frameworks and the impact of these on local regulatory frameworks.
Provide highly specialised and detailed regulatory frameworks on prudential and joint regulation in the context of the FSR Act as well as on the regulatory framework for financial market infrastructures.
Draft prudential and joint regulatory frameworks and instruments.
Influence international standard-setting bodies and possibly represent the PA at such forums.
Represent the PA in Parliament and other forums on policy matters and legislation.
Adopt and adapt international standards to South African requirements to be purpose-specific to South Africa, yet compliant with international criteria.
Be a custodian of the PA policy process and related governance processes.
Coordinate and complete international surveys.
Brief divisional heads, heads of department, the PA Chief Executive Officer (CEO), the SARB Executive and other stakeholders on policy positions on specialised topics.
Represent the PA at various public forums (e.g. training, lecturing and presenting at conferences).
Coordinate the PA ‘house view’ in collaboration with the SARB and other regulators.
Conduct and coordinate ad hoc research on prudential-related topics.
Draft speeches and similar documents for the PA CEO, the SARB Executive on specialist topics.
Prepare briefings on specialist topics for the PA CEO, the SARB Executive as well as heads of department to represent the PA/SARB view locally and internationally.
Take ownership and responsibility for the implementation of microprudential matters within the mandate of the PA and in support of the financial stability mandate of the SARB.
Job requirements
To be considered for this position, candidates must be in possession of:
a postgraduate degree (NQF 9) in Law, Finance, Accounting, Insurance, Banking or Economics; and
12 years’ experience in the financial markets infrastructure sector, financial sector or risk management environment.
Additional requirements include:
knowledge of:
the financial sector, specifically financial markets infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk;
corporate governance and risk management practices and standards;
international principles on financial market infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk;
principles for financial market infrastructures;
insurance core principles, solvency II or solvency assessment and management;
banking core principles and Basel III;the financial sector, specifically financial markets infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk;
corporate governance and risk management practices and standards;
international principles on financial market infrastructures, structured debt instruments and market risk;
principles for financial market infrastructures;
insurance core principles, solvency II or solvency assessment and management;
banking core principles and Basel III;
problem-solving and analytical skills;
planning and organising skills;
verbal and written communication skills;
strong presentation skills;
financial and business acumen;
team player;
self-starter;
attention to detail; and
confidentiality.
